---
description: >
  Conventions for the local UnrealMCP tools: asset paths vs actor labels,
  batching, read-before-write, undo, and verification.
alwaysApply: true
---

Asset paths use `/Package/Path.AssetName` (e.g. `/Game/Blueprints/BP_Enemy.BP_Enemy`, class: `/Game/Blueprints/BP_Enemy.BP_Enemy_C`). Get real paths from the asset registry (via `execute_python`) or tool output. Never guess them.

Actor labels (`get_actors_in_level`, `find_actors_by_name`) are NOT asset paths. Reflection tools accept either an object path (starting with `/`) or an actor label.

Read before write: inspect the target (`object_describe`, `read_blueprint_content`, `get_actors_in_level`) before modifying it.

Batch: for many similar edits, write one `execute_python` loop. Don't make one call per item.

Undo: every mutating command is one editor transaction, so `editor_action(action="undo")` reverts it. In `execute_python`, wrap edits in `with unreal.ScopedEditorTransaction("...")`.

Look up before you code: run `unreal_api_search` before `execute_python` whenever you aren't certain of an API name.

Verify after every modification: re-read the state, check `editor_log(since_seq=..., min_verbosity="Warning")`, and use `viewport_capture` for visual work. "No error" is not proof that the change worked.
